Next, we need to define update function somewhere after loop. At appropriate section, we can add // === UPDATE section after loop.

Define function update(dt) { // player movement, tilt handling, maybe auto move
   if (phase !== "playing") return;
   // Update player position based on tiltDirection and speeds.
   player.x += tiltDirection * PLAYER_SPEED_X * dt;
   player.y += PLAYER_SPEED_Y * dt;
   // Keep player inside bounds
   if (player.x < 0) player.x = 0;
   if (player.x > canvas.width) player.x = canvas.width;
   // Check floor (lose condition)
   if (player.y > canvas.height + player.radius) {
      phase = "gameover";
      // beep on death
      beep(110, 0.25);
   }
   // Increment score over time (or per frame)
   score += Math.round(dt * 10); // 10 points per second
   // or maybe count frames
 }

But need to define tiltDirection variable globally (so we should add in state or config). Add const TILT_DIRECTION_INITIAL = 1? Actually we can define let tiltDirection = 1; in STATE section after keys set.

Thus edit state section: after let keys = new Set(); insert let tiltDirection = 1;

Now modify reset function to set tiltDirection = 1.

Also define render function after loop: draws background, player, HUD.

Render: fill background (maybe fillRect with color #0f1320). Draw player as a circle maybe color #7df9ff. Draw score text.

Add with ctx.fillStyle.

We also need to check that we already call ctx.clearRect at top; can still draw background but not necessary; but we implement anyway.

Define publishState function that sets window.__game accordingly: state is "playing" etc. phase variable "menu", "playing", "gameover". When resetting we set phase "playing". At initial boot, we should start in "menu"? Not sure. Could set phase = "menu", and then startLoop after calling reset? Actually reset sets phase "playing" and calls startLoop, so initial boot we might want to call reset() to start playing directly. No menu needed.

Thus window.__game state: if phase == "playing" then "playing", else if phase == "gameover" then "gameover", else "menu". Score and player known.

player visible: true if player is within canvas (0 <= x <= canvas.width && 0 <= y <= canvas.height); compute each call.

Define function publishState() { window.__game = { state: phase === "playing" ? "playing" : phase === "gameover" ? "gameover" : "menu", score, player: { x: player.x, y: player.y, visible: player.x >= 0 && player.x <= canvas.width && player.y >= 0 && player.y <= canvas.height }, objective: OBJECTIVE }; }

Now we need to define beep function similar to reference.

Add // === AUDIO (lazy) ======================================================= after audio context variable perhaps.

Define function beep(freq, durSec) { try { if (!audioCtx) audioCtx = new (window.AudioContext || window.webkitAudioContext)(); const osc = audioCtx.createOscillator(); const gain = audioCtx.createGain(); osc.type = "square"; osc.frequency.value = freq; gain.gain.setValueAtTime(0.05, audioCtx.currentTime); gain.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.001, audioCtx.currentTime + durSec); osc.connect(gain); gain.connect(audioCtx.destination); osc.start(); osc.stop(audioCtx.currentTime + durSec); } catch {} }

Now input handling: // === INPUT =============================================================. Add event listeners:

document.addEventListener('keydown', (ev) => { if (ev.key === 'r' || ev.key === 'R') { reset(); return; } if (ev.key === ' ') { tiltDirection *= -1; beep(440, 0.1); } keys.add(ev.key); });
document.addEventListener('keyup', (ev) => { keys.delete(ev.key); });
restartBtn.addEventListener('click', reset);
canvas.addEventListener('click', (ev) => { tiltDirection *= -1; beep(440, 0.1); });

Note: Use canvas click not document click to avoid toggling on UI.

Finally BOOT: // === BOOT =============================================================== and call reset() to start.

Add at bottom after all definitions but before closing script.

